# kb-code bench-search starter set — the kb repo itself (W5.5).
#
# One JSON object per line: {"query": "...", "expect_path": "...",
# "expect_kind": "..."}. `expect_kind` only labels the human table's
# grouping (symbol/filename/semantic/error-string) — it never gates
# scoring. Every `query` uses kb-code's own search grammar (@sym, #file,
# bare = every lane, `?nl ` = semantic, trailing `path:<substr>` to
# disambiguate a string that appears in more than one file) — see
# `kb_code_server::search::grammar`'s module doc.
#
# Run against a kb-code daemon configured with this repo itself as a
# browsable repo, e.g.: `kb-code bench-search --repo kb --queries
# crates/kb-code-cli/bench/kb-repo-queries.jsonl`.
#
# Paths/symbols/strings below were verified against the real source at
# HEAD as of the W5.3+W5.5 commit — a later refactor can legitimately move
# one of these and drop its recall; that's the bench doing its job, not a
# bug in the query set.
#
# ROOT-CAUSE NOTE (dogfood run 2026-07-20, sessions 0/0, transcripts 0/0):
# neither lane can EVER score a hit under this file's rows, and that is
# STRUCTURAL, not a stale expect_path or a matcher bug to fix here.
# `bench::hit_rank` (crates/kb-code-cli/src/bench.rs) matches a lane hit
# purely by its `path` field. `search::sessions::SessionHit` and
# `transcripts::search::TranscriptHit` (crates/kb-code-server/src/
# search/sessions.rs, .../transcripts/search.rs) carry NO `path` field at
# all — sessions hits are {session_id, title, score, started_at, source,
# files_changed}, transcript hits are {session_id, uuid, ts, kind,
# tool_name, project_dir, snippet, is_sidechain}. Every hit either lane
# ever returns is therefore an automatic miss, independent of query
# quality — bench.rs's own module doc ("Scoring") already says as much
# ("lanes whose hits carry no `path` field, e.g. sessions/transcripts,
# never match"). No row below targets either lane on purpose (there is no
# `~`/`~~`-prefixed row in this file); they only show up in the table
# because the four bare/unprefixed rows (the `kb-code-why.sh` filename row
# and the three `error-string` rows below) fan out to EVERY lane per
# `search::grammar`'s "no prefix = every lane" rule. Until `bench.rs`
# grows a session/transcript-shaped match key (e.g. against `snippet`/
# `title`/`session_id` instead of `path`), do not read a 0% row for either
# lane as a retrieval regression, and do not "fix" it by editing
# `expect_path` here — no path value can ever match.
#
# The sessions lane ALSO has a live-daemon dependency, verified flaky
# in the same investigation: `search::sessions::search` federates a real
# HTTP call to the OPERATOR'S OWN `kb` daemon (not kb-code) at
# `[kb_daemon] url` (default `http://127.0.0.1:4000`), 1.5s timeout. A
# repeated identical query against a live kb-code daemon returned
# `unavailable_reason: "kb daemon unreachable ... error sending request"`
# on 1 of 3 attempts (the other 2 succeeded in well under a second) — so
# the sessions lane's reported `n` in a bench-search run is itself
# non-deterministic run over run (it drops out of scoring entirely on a
# miss, per bench.rs's `unavailable_reason` skip rule), on top of always
# reading 0% recall. Requires: a reachable `kb` daemon at `[kb_daemon]
# url` for the sessions lane to be present in the table at all; even then
# its recall column is uninformative per the structural note above.

